<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Serilog rather than the console logger's built-in JSON formatter, which would have cost no
    packages at all. The two write different field names (CLEF's @t/@mt/@l against Timestamp,
    LogLevel, Message), and this process's output is read interleaved with the API's in one
    `docker compose logs`, where two shapes is a cost paid on every reading. Versions are pinned
    to the same Serilog release train Mcs.Api is on, so the repository has one answer to "which
    Serilog".
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.10" />

    <!--
      ValidateDataAnnotations lives here rather than in Microsoft.Extensions.Options. Mcs.Api gets
      it for free from the ASP.NET Core shared framework, which is why the station's Program.cs
      names no such package; a console host has to ask.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.DataAnnotations" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Formatting.Compact" Version="3.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Settings.Configuration" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Console" Version="6.1.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Mcs.Adapters, for MavlinkFrameWriter and nothing else. That is the ticket's point: the codec
    gets a second consumer that is not a test, and the frames this process emits are framed by the
    same code the station un-frames them with.

    Note what is deliberately not shared. MavlinkMessageId and the four message structs are
    internal to Mcs.Adapters and stay that way. The payload writers under Mavlink/ here are
    written independently of the station's readers, so a field offset or a byte order wrong in one
    cannot cancel against the same mistake in the other. An InternalsVisibleTo pointing this way
    would have saved four small files and destroyed the only thing the decode test proves.

    Mcs.Core is deliberately absent. This is a vehicle, not a station: no telemetry model, no
    store, no ingest boundary. It arrives transitively and nothing here may name it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Mcs.Adapters\Mcs.Adapters.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The flight model and the payload writers are the parts with arithmetic in them, and the parts
    a test has to reach directly: a turn radius is asserted against a closed-form answer, not
    inferred from the bytes that eventually came out of a socket.
  -->
  <!--
    The console SDK copies nothing to the output directory by default, unlike the Web one, and
    Host.CreateApplicationBuilder reads appsettings.json from the content root. Without this line
    the process starts, finds no Simulator section, and fails startup validation on an empty route:
    a confusing way to be told a file was not deployed.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="appsettings.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Mcs.Simulator.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
